      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, I'd be happy to help. Teaching high school students about systemic racism in housing and banking systems can truly illustrate the widespread effects these social constructs have.\n\n**Teaching Guide: Understanding Systemic Racism in Housing and Banking**\n\n1. **Introduction (10 minutes)**\n   - Begin by explaining what systemic racism means and how it relates to housing and banking.\n   - Show a short, engaging documentary excerpt to visually demonstrate these concepts.\n\n2. **Housing System Structures (15 minutes)**\n   - Discuss the historical context of redlining (1934 Housing Act) and its lasting impact on urban development.\n   - Use maps and images to show zoning laws and their effects on predominantly African American neighborhoods.\n   - Highlight modern-day housing policies and practices that perpetuate inequality.\n\n3. **Banking System Structures (15 minutes)**\n   - Explain terms like mortgage rates, predatory lending, and credit discrimination.\n   - Present real-world scenarios or case studies (like the Freddie Mac scandal) to discuss how banking practices can disadvantage people of color.\n   - Show statistical graphs to present disparities in home ownership and loan approvals among racial groups.\n\n4. **Interactive Discussion (20 minutes)**\n   - Facilitate a student-led discussion: \"What structures do",
